# Workflow Tips

## Break up work into stacked PRs

Don't land large changes as one giant PR. Split work into small, reviewable,
stacked PRs using `gh`:

- Use the `split-to-prs` skill to plan the slices before creating branches.
- Order slices so foundations land before consumers (e.g. shared types before
  the UI/API code that uses them).
- Each PR in the stack branches off the previous PR's branch, not `main`:

  ```bash
  git checkout -b feat/part-1 main
  # ...commit slice 1...
  git push -u origin feat/part-1
  gh pr create --base main --head feat/part-1

  git checkout -b feat/part-2 feat/part-1
  # ...commit slice 2...
  git push -u origin feat/part-2
  gh pr create --base feat/part-1 --head feat/part-2
  ```

- As earlier PRs in the stack merge, retarget the next PR's base with
  `gh pr edit <number> --base <new-base>` and rebase.
- Default to independent PRs off `main`; only stack when there's a real
  dependency between slices.

## Always run the full test suite

Never consider a feature or fix done without actually running the tests —
don't assume they pass:

- UI: `pnpm --filter ui test` (vitest)
- API: `cd api && make test` (go test ./...)

Run both, not just the workspace you think you touched, and report the real
pass/fail results. Use the `develop` subagent (`.cursor/agents/develop.md`)
for the full implement → format → lint → test → review flow, triggered by
"start work on ...".

## Always reference the original site for design

This is a rebuild of [mariamecoulibaly.com](https://www.mariamecoulibaly.com/).
For any layout, styling, animation, copy, or content work:

- Check the live site to confirm exact copy, spacing, colors, fonts, and
  interaction/animation behavior (hover states, transitions, timing) before
  implementing — don't approximate from memory or guesswork.
- Preserve existing project slugs and page structure for URL/SEO parity
  (see `docs/PLAN.md`).
- When in doubt about a design detail, fetch the relevant live page and
  compare side-by-side rather than assuming.
